    whatToExpect: [
      'A MacBook Pro is usually somebody\'s working machine, so the first thing we establish is how quickly you need it back and whether the repair can be done in front of you. Every visit starts with a free diagnosis at your home or office: what has actually failed, whether it is worth repairing, and a fixed price for parts and labour before any work begins. If the honest answer is that the repair does not make economic sense against the age of the machine, we say so.',
      'The Pro line has its own well-known weak points and we see them constantly. Butterfly keyboards on the 2016–2019 models that repeat or drop keys. Touch Bars that stop responding. Flexgate display cables. Thermal throttling on the higher-end Intel 15-inch and 16-inch machines, where dried-out paste and a fan full of dust turn a fast Mac into a slow, loud one. Batteries that swell and lift the trackpad. Most of these are fixed on-site in a single visit.',
      'Board-level work is where a MacBook Pro genuinely needs a workshop rather than a kitchen table. Liquid damage, a Mac that shows no sign of life, a failed charging circuit or a short on the board all need a microscope, proper rework equipment and time. We take those away, keep you updated, and return the machine fully tested. Around three in ten of our jobs go this route; the rest are finished in your home or office.',
      'On the M1, M2 and M3 Pros there is one thing worth knowing upfront: memory and storage are soldered to the board, so they cannot be upgraded later and a storage failure is a board-level job. That makes a current backup more important on Apple Silicon than it ever was on the Intel machines. Whatever the repair, it carries our 90-day parts-and-labour warranty, there is no callout charge, and nothing to pay if we cannot fix it.',
    ],
    faqs: [
      { q: 'Which MacBook Pro models do you repair?', a: 'All of them — the M1, M2 and M3 Pro and Max machines in 13, 14 and 16-inch, and every Intel MacBook Pro before them including the 2016–2019 Touch Bar generation and the older non-Retina models. Tell us the model and year when you call and we will bring the right parts.' },
      { q: 'My MacBook Pro keyboard is repeating or dropping keys — can that be fixed?', a: 'Yes, and it is one of the most common Pro faults we see. The butterfly mechanism on the 2016–2019 models is genuinely fragile and a single crumb can disable a key. Depending on the model this is either a keyboard replacement or a top-case replacement, and we will tell you which yours needs and what it costs before starting.' },
      { q: 'My MacBook Pro is loud and slow under load — is it broken?', a: 'Usually not broken, just choking. The higher-end Intel 15 and 16-inch machines throttle hard once the thermal paste has dried out and the fans and heatsink are clogged with dust. A full strip-down, clean and re-paste brings the performance back and quietens it down, and it is one of the more transformative jobs we do on an older Pro.' },
      { q: 'Can you upgrade the RAM or storage in my MacBook Pro?', a: 'On the Intel models with removable drives, often yes. On any M1, M2 or M3 Pro, no — memory and storage are soldered directly to the logic board, and nobody can upgrade them afterwards. We will confirm exactly what your model supports before quoting, so you never pay for an upgrade that physically cannot fit.' },
      { q: 'Is a Touch Bar failure worth repairing?', a: 'It depends what else is going on with the machine. The Touch Bar assembly is a real part with a real cost, and on a 2017 Pro that is also on its third battery the sums may not work. We will diagnose it free, quote it honestly, and tell you where we think the line is rather than just taking the job.' },
      { q: 'How quickly can you repair a MacBook Pro?', a: 'Same-day callouts across Greater London if you book before 2pm, including evenings and weekends at no extra charge. On-site repairs — batteries, keyboards, screens, thermal work, software — are usually finished in that one visit. Board-level work goes to the workshop and comes back as fast as the parts allow.' },
    ],
